events to remember those killed 70 years  ago and the veterans who have died since  have a particular poignancy this  anniversary the war began on the 25th of  June 1950 with the surprise attack by  troops across the border that separated  North and South Korea around noon people  started flooding into town carrying kids  on their backs telling us the North had  invaded then artillery shells began  falling downtown some of these veterans  were still at high school at the time  but were quickly recruited into the army  as the South reeled from the attack they  showed us how to shoot a rifle a few  times and then send us up a hill to go  into battle we spend the next three  months in the trenches the war lasted  three years with millions of Chinese  troops supporting the north and a  multinational UN force led by the u.s.  supporting the south to this day there's  been no formal end to the war only an  armistice in spite of attempts to secure  won most recently during improved  inter-korean relations peace is a right  that we Korean citizens deserve to  relish making a peaceful Korean  Peninsula with no chance of yet another  war is a duty bestowed by the people  upon the state estimates vary but as  many as 3 million people are thought to  have died in the Korean War around half  of them civilians  that's a disproportionately heavy toll  that means the war's legacy is still  felt just as keenly 7 decades on in both  Korea's north and south surviving  veterans in the South complain of  insufficient pensions and many feel  abandoned by the country they helped  save it was our sweat and sacrifice that  led to victory and to the remarkable  development of our country his more time  has passed that's become a forgotten war  the appreciation for those who fought  and were injured has faded a lot and  with each year there are fewer to  remember firsthand what happened as  their generation fades  Rob McBride al-jazeera so
 

